* means corrected

1) Additional element of a block can hide a cursor.
2)* If part of long hierarchy of radicals disappears on the right,
anything disappears. (* Was: wrong interaction of wrapped lines with
tcl layout. *)
3) Algorithm for layout can become exponential if it tries to wrap
around n levels of IBTrees to fit into space.
4) Change ArgAppendCopy to ArgAppendShadow? (incr refcount instead of copy?)
5)* (2.00) Text indices counted bytes; Perl/Tk 804 stores text as UTF-8,
so a non-ASCII character occupied several index positions ("1.0+1c"
after a 3-byte character was in the middle of it).  (* Indices count
characters now, inside blocks too; t/utf8.t compares with Tk::Text. *)
6) (2.00) The Tcl/Tk flavour of the sources is unmaintained.
7)* (2.00) "search" did not skip elided text; undoing the deletion of
a block restored its text, not the block; "dump" did not look into
blocks.  (* All three done, the undo of a deletion that cuts a block
included; undo re-creates embedded images and windows too.  A window
given with -window was destroyed with the deletion, as in Tk, and
comes back as an empty window segment. *)
8)* (2.00) Deleting all of a block except its head, or all except its
tail (which deletes the block, as documented), crashed.  (* Fixed. *)
9)* (2.00) An embedded window inside a block could not be addressed by
name or by index: "index" by its name gave the position inside the
block, "window cget" and "window configure" looked on the outer line
only.  (* Fixed. *)
10) (2.00) Display lines never span logical lines (an elided newline
does not merge two lines, as it does in Tk 8.5), and a space at the
right margin needs a pixel of room to stay on its line (Tk 8.4's rule;
Tk 8.5 lets it hang over).  Known differences from Tk 8.5, kept.
11)* (2.03) The redo of an insertion re-inserts its text without the
tags it was inserted with, as Tk does: a run inside it does not come
back, and its value (kept by the undo of the insertion) is freed at
the redo.  (* 2.04: the undo of an insertion records the inserted
range in block-list form and the redo inserts that list: the tags,
the separators in a block, the marks and the values come back. *)
12)* (2.03) "tag add" and "tag remove" are not undoable, as in Tk: the
values of runs they make lose a merge, or remove, are freed when they
return instead of being kept for an undo.  The same for "block
insert" and "block trim", which are not undoable either.  (* 2.04:
they are undoable, and keep what they drop in their undo steps; see
18 for what block insert's undo cannot re-create. *)
13) (2.03) Once a node value has been set in a widget, every undoable
deletion takes a snapshot of the node values of the blocks it
overlaps, even after all the values are gone (a cost limited to those
blocks; a count kept per tree would have to follow every structural
change of the trees).
14)* (2.03) Long random sequences of block editing and undo (deletions,
insertions, undo, redo, block split, block insert) crash in the
B-tree on some sequences (with TkBTreeCheck on, "CharCheckProc:
segment has size <= 0" after an undo); release 2.02 does the same
with no values set: a defect of block editing and undo, not of the
values.  (* Fixed, ten causes, each with its minimal script in
t/stress.t: block split did not tell the display about its newline;
a deletion from the tail of a nested block left its split in the line
for the B-tree check to find; an -empty block deleted inside a block
was taken off the block's length twice; "block list" (the undo record)
from just after a block reported a piece of it; undo could re-create a
block on the dummy line; a reversed range of block insert; an
undefined index; the tag counts of nodes glued or merged by a deletion
in a block; nodes left without children by the deletions that dissolve
a block.  The stress scripts and t/stress.t's random runs no longer
crash. *)
15)* (2.03) "block insert" of an -empty block at the start of a line
crashes (inside a line or at its end it works); release 2.02 does the
same.  (* Fixed: an empty range is split once. *)
16)* (2.03) "block split" is not recorded for undo either (see 12): an
undo after a block split, or after a block insert, applies the earlier
changes at their recorded indices, which the split or the block has
moved, so it changes the wrong place (it no longer crashes).  (* 2.04:
block split, insert and trim are recorded and undone. *)
17)* (2.04) STOPGAP: while the values a widget command dropped are being
freed (their DESTROY methods run when the command returns), the widget
refuses every command that would modify it ("cannot modify .t while
its values are being freed"); reading commands work, the queries of
tag configure, tag bind and block configure included when the tag or
block type exists (they would create one otherwise, and are refused
then).  The manual lists the commands refused.  Postponed: a DESTROY method may need to inspect
where in the block its value was attached, and it runs when the value
is already detached, which may be too late.  The planned fix is a
notification BEFORE the value is detached, giving its location (a
node: index and level; a mark: name; a run: tag and range).  2.05: the
notification is there, the option -valuecommand (manual, "Telling the
program: -valuecommand"): before a command changes anything (before
each change for the commands that make several, see 27), the
callback is told which values the change will detach (drop, stash,
move) and where they are, while the widget is unchanged; afterwards
which come back (restore) and which a discarded undo step frees
(discard).  A detach that was not foreseen is reported "late", an
error under "debug"; t/valuecommand-random.t finds none.  (* 2.05:
fixed; with the place told in time, the stopgap is gone: only the
-valuecommand callback is refused changes (the command that called it
has not made its own yet).  A DESTROY method may change the widget
while values are freed; such a change is an undo step of its own, the
values it drops are freed in the same pass, and a chain of such
changes stops 10 deep (TK_TEXT_FREE_DEPTH).  t/freeing.t.  Then the
limit became the owner's DESTROYmult: at the limit the object whose
DESTROY makes the change is asked $obj->DESTROYmult($n, $limit), and
a positive integer raises the limit by that much (manual, "When values
are freed").  t/destroymult.t. *)
18)* (2.04) The undo of a "block insert" whose range crossed nodes of
an enclosing block (see the manual) gives back the text, tags, marks,
leaves and leaf values, but not the node boundaries the range crossed:
the leaves come back as children of the node where the range started,
and the values of the nodes block insert emptied are freed.  (* 2.05:
fixed; the undo step records the level of each separator in the range
and of the one after it (TkIBTreeSeparatorLevels), and the undo, once
it has dissolved the block, sets each back to its level, splitting or
joining nodes without moving text (TkIBTreeSetSeparatorLevels), before
the values come back to their nodes.  t/undo-block.t; the review's
random undo/redo exactness check passes on all of its 300 runs. *)
19)* (2.03) Found by a random undo exactness check: the undo of a
deletion could put a tag toggle or an -empty block on the other side
of a block's head, or give a re-created block's head or tail tags it
did not have, when the deleted range had blocks in runs of tags.  (*
2.04: fixed; the causes were in the re-creation of the deleted text
from its list: a reused temporary mark name, and the head and tail of
a re-created block keeping the tags of the run it was made in.  The
review's fuzzer and the exactness check find none any more.  Also
fixed: the undo gave the value of a run that started where the
deletion started, or in the remains of a block it dissolved, to
another run of the tag. *)
20)* (2.03) "tag names" at a block's head leaves out every tag that
toggles somewhere inside the block (TkIBTreeFixTags, the original
design: the head counts as tagged only by tags constant over the
block), while "tag ranges", "dump" and the searches count the head as
tagged when the tag is on just before it.  So with b on 1.0-1.3 over
a{bcd}e, "tag names 1.1" is empty though 1.1 is inside the range 1.0
1.3.  Not changed: TkTextIsElided and the display take the head's tags
from the same function, and a block partly elided would change how it
is drawn.  Reproducer: the review's round2/tagnames-head.pl.  (* 2.05:
fixed; "tag names" has a query of its own (TkBTreeGetTagsInEffect)
that reports the tags in effect at the head, and agrees with "tag
ranges" everywhere (t/regress.t).  The display, the elide test and
the mouse bindings keep the old rule (TkBTreeGetTags): a tag that
switches off inside a block does not style the block as a whole. *)
21)* Under Wine (Strawberry Perl 5.38 and the patched Perl/Tk, see
HANDOVER section 2), t/demo.t sometimes failed before its first test
(after its plan): "wine: Unhandled page fault on read access to
FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF at address 00006FFFFA2595B8", exit 5; 12 of 100 runs
of t/demo.t alone (the earlier "never alone" was chance), 2.03 too.
Cause: a bug of Perl/Tk 804.036 on every platform, not of eText
(TK_BUGS.md W7 in the repository).  GetOptionFromObj (pTk tkConfig.c)
caches its lookup in the option-name SV, as the address of the option
table and of the entry found; the tables belong to a MainWindow and are
freed when it is destroyed, the SV keeps the cache, and when a table of
a later MainWindow lands at the same address the stale entry is used:
wrong cget results, or a crash.  Every MainWindow->new calls
cget('-background') with the same literal (Tk/Derived.pm:339), and
t/demo.t destroyed a probe MainWindow before the demo created its own.
The address is in Tk.xs.dll, which Wine loads at 0x6FFFFA250000:
Perl_SvREFCNT_inc called from Tk_GetOptionValue.  Not seen in t/demo.t
on Linux, but the reproducer (upstream/perltk-W7-option-cache-repro.pl
in the repository) shows the bug there too.  (* Worked around in
t/demo.t (2.05): the probe is withdrawn, and destroyed after the demo's
MainWindow, so no MainWindow is created after one was destroyed (with
the probe kept, 0 failures in 60 runs under Wine, against 5 of 60 for
the old test run in between).  Fixed in Perl/Tk by
upstream/perltk-W7-option-cache.patch (written up for Perl/Tk as
upstream/reports/16-perltk-option-cache.md, not filed yet):
with it, the old t/demo.t ran 40 times under Wine without a failure.
A program that creates a MainWindow after destroying one needs the
patched Perl/Tk. *)
22)* (2.04) "block data" replacing the instance data of a block frees
the old value at once, inside the widget command (Tk_ConfigureWidget
did it for the TK_CONFIG_LANGARG option; 2.05 keeps it in
BlockDataParse, eText/tkText.c), and not when the command returns as
every other value: its DESTROY method runs while the command is going
on, and the stopgap of 17 does not apply (it may modify the widget).
Reproducer: blockData with an object whose DESTROY calls insert, then
blockData again: the insert is allowed, while the same done with
blockNodedata is refused.  (* 2.05: fixed; the value replaced goes to
the list of values freed when the command ends, so its DESTROY runs
under the stopgap, still before blockData returns.  t/freeing.t. *)
23)* (2.04) The undo of a deletion keeps the instance data of the blocks
it deleted or dissolved as copies in its block list (the node and run
values are records instead): when the undo step is discarded (edit
reset, a new change clearing the redo stack, -maxundo), FreeAtom
releases the list at once, so the DESTROY method of such instance
data runs inside the widget command, not deferred, and the stopgap of
17 does not apply.  For the same reason, with the representation B of
2.05 the slots other than the Perl value of such instance data do
not come back with the undo (tkTextData.h).  (* 2.05: fixed; the
undo's block list leaves the instance data out, as it does node, run
and mark values, and the undo step keeps the whole container as a
record addressed by the block's head; the undo gives it back to the
re-created block, and a discarded step frees it when the command
ends, under the stopgap.  The same for the redo of an insertion,
which re-inserts from a block list too.  t/freeing.t, t/undo-block.t,
t/data-slots.t. *)
24)* (2.05) A tag with -elide on that started before a block and ended
inside it hid the whole block and the rest of the line, text the tag
does not cover included (search still found that text): with ab
{cdefgh} ij, the block at 1.3, and the tag on 1.0-1.6, nothing of the
line was drawn, though "efgh" and " ij" are not tagged.  The display
skips elided text in two loops (LayoutDLine, eText/tkTextDisp.c: the
whole-line fast path and the elided-chunk loop) that passed over a
block without looking at the toggles inside it.  A range starting at
the head was right: the block is laid out with the style of its head,
which leaves out a tag that switches inside it (BUGS 20), and hides
what the tag covers inside.  (* Fixed: both loops stop at a block
inside which a tag with -elide switches (TkIBTreeTogglesElide) and let
it be laid out like that.  t/elide.t compares what is drawn with the
tag's ranges, for ranges starting before, at and inside blocks and
ending inside or after them. *)
25)* (2.04) An -empty block did not know its line any more once it had
moved to another one (a newline inserted before it, a block split
before it, block insert moving it into a new block): the line of a
block is updated when the block's segment is cleaned up after the
move, and that was done only for blocks with contents.  "block at" on
the head of such a block crashed (the HANDOVER's reproducer: an -empty
block nested in a block by block insert), and anything else that asks
for the position of its head would have.  (* 2.05: fixed; the B-tree
check, under "debug", now checks that each block knows its line.
t/regress.t. *)
26)* (2.04) A value set, after a deletion that dissolved a block, on a
leaf made of what was left of the block was freed by the undo of the
deletion (instead of waiting in the undo step, as a value set after a
change on a node the undo gives a value back to does): the undo takes
the remains of the cut blocks out with their values before
re-inserting the deleted text, and gives the values back at the same
places in the restored text, where such a leaf does not exist.  Found
by the random test of -valuecommand (the value was freed without
notice).  (* Fixed: such a value waits in the undo step with its
place in the text without the deleted range, where the redo gives it
back.  t/undo-block.t. *)
27) (2.05) POSTPONED: -valuecommand was approved as telling the program
everything "before the command changes anything".  The commands that
make several changes in a row tell before EACH change instead, with
the widget as the previous change left it (so $where names the place
at that moment): edit undo and edit redo (atom by atom), insert with
lists of tags (each piece's tags taken off and put on), tag add and
tag remove over several ranges, and a deletion through the end of the
text (which first takes the tags off the final newline).  A value an
undo or a redo displaces is told just before it moves, and "restore"
comes as soon as a value is back.  The owner postponed the question;
telling everything at the start would need the look-ahead of all the
changes of such a command from the widget as it is before the first.
The manual's "Telling the program: -valuecommand" has the note.
28) (2.06: ragged trees) What is temporary in trees whose leaves are
at different depths (block sprout, block flatten) and in blocks that
are a single leaf (block flatten at the root): choices made for now,
to be reconsidered, probably by letting callbacks choose (the design
note RAGGED-ATOMIC.md, section 8, on the branch ragged-design).  The
undo and redo of the structural edits are exact: a deletion that joined two leaves of different
depths records the WRAPs it made first (the nodes given to the
shallower leaf), its undo re-creates the text with every leaf at its
depth (the undo's lists carry the number of padding nodes above each
leaf they re-create: the pairs [depth, defect] of the separators of
"block list", public since decision L1) and then pulls the WRAPs out
again; the undo of "block insert" gives the leaves of its range their
depths back with the levels of the separators; block flatten is undone
by the inverse of its steps in reverse order (a JOIN is recorded as the
deletion of its separator, a PULL OUT as an atom of its own); a change
that gives a block that was a single leaf a real root (a newline in
the leaf, block split, block sprout, a nested block dissolved in it)
records it, and its undo makes the root padding again (t/ragged.t,
t/flatten.t, t/stress.t and t/valuecommand-random.t check the block
list with the values and the shape of every block before, after,
after the undo and after the redo).  What remains:
- Which sequence of atomic steps an edit uses (RAGGED-ATOMIC.md 8.1),
  chosen for now: block flatten JOINs the rightmost separator inside
  the node whose two leaves are equally deep below their common
  ancestor, else PULLs OUT the deepest node with a single child that
  is a leaf (rightmost among equals), then the node itself; a deletion
  across depths WRAPs the shallower leaf, never PULLs OUT.  The
  shortest sequence is not unique: the shape is the same for every
  flatten, the values that survive are not.  To reconsider, probably
  with callbacks that choose.
- The value rules of the atomic steps (8.2): a JOIN keeps the start
  side's value, else takes the end side's (U1); a PULL OUT gives the
  leaf the node's value if it has one, else the leaf keeps its own
  (U2); block sprout shares the former leaf's value between the start
  piece, the new node and the end piece.  Also to reconsider.
- -valuecommand tells the WRAPs, PULL OUTs and JOINs of ragged trees
  before each step (as the commands of 27), with the new place as it
  is after the step, and the copies of "block sprout" as "copy"; the
  undo of a deletion across depths tells the values coming back
  ("restore") at their places in the tree as the WRAPs left it, then
  each PULL OUT ("move" of the nodes renumbered).  The nodes the steps
  make without a value are told "new" (below).  One value shared by
  several owners (block sprout) is one entry for the look-ahead: each
  owner is told, and any of the events foreseen for it is accepted.
- A value set, after such a deletion, on the node a WRAP made above the
  start leaf waits in the undo step (as every value set on a place the
  undo takes away) and comes back with the redo; the same for a value
  set on the root a change gave a block that was a single leaf.  A
  value set on the joined node when the end side's node had one (which
  the join gave to the start side) goes, with the undo, to the end
  side's node, as in a uniform tree; the same for a value set on the
  leaf a PULL OUT gave the node's value to: the undo gives it to the
  node.
- "block insert" across leaves of different depths does not equalize
  them first (the design's "any other edit that joins leaves
  equalizes"): it joins no nodes, the rest of the last leaf goes into
  the first leaf, which keeps its depth; the end leaf's nodes that
  lose their last line go.  Its undo is exact.
- The event "new" of -valuecommand (decision O5, option A, added on
  2026-11-11, PROVISIONAL): told before the change, once for each node
  or leaf the change creates without a value, with its place after
  the change ([index, level], level counted from the node's first
  leaf) and the value undef: the new leaves of newlines typed in a
  block and of "block split", the second halves of the nodes block
  split cuts and, where it reaches the root, the nodes it first
  inserts between the root and all its children (the root stays the
  root with its value; its address grows by a level, told "move", as
  for a WRAP); the root and the leaves of a new block (block
  insert) without a value, and the enclosing leaf left when a whole
  leaf moves into the new block; the leaves that the separators of a
  block a deletion dissolves make in the block around it; the middle
  leaf of "block sprout", its new node and its start and end pieces
  when they receive no copy (the former leaf had no value), and the
  root a single-leaf block gets; the nodes a deletion across depths
  gives the start leaf (those of the end leaf, merged into the start
  side's at once, are not told); the root of a single-leaf block made
  real by a newline, a split or a dissolved block.  Decided for now:
  an undo or a redo tells no "new" (what it re-creates gets back the
  value it had, "restore"; a node without a value it re-creates or
  removes is not told), and the disappearance of a node without a
  value is never told.  "block sprout" tells all its "new" events
  before its first step, with the places after the command; a
  deletion tells those of its WRAPs before them, with the places once
  all are made.  The owner's example in the request named the new
  root of "block split 2.2 1" on {num|den} as new at [2.1 2]; the
  widget tells [2.1 1]: the split inserts a node R1 between the root
  R and all its children (R stays the root and keeps its value; its
  address moves from [2.1 1] to [2.1 2], told "move"), then splits R1
  into X( n ) and Y( um | den ), and R1 (= X) is the node told new at
  [2.1 1].  t/valuecommand-random.t checks
  that every node created is told (every node has a value before each
  command in half of its widgets), t/ragged.t and t/flatten.t the
  places.
- "block sprout index" with one index (decision O8, 2.06) wraps the
  leaf the index names (also the only leaf of a block, which the
  two-index form cannot name: that range belongs to the enclosing
  structure); the leaf's value moves to the new node and the leaf is
  told "new", as for the two-index form from delimiter to delimiter.
29)* (2.01) Found by the random undo exactness check of 2.06: the undo
of a deletion inside a block re-created a separator of a level above 0
(a "block split" at that level) with the tags of the place by the
insertion rule, also those the deleted range had off on it: with x on
"ab" and "cd" of {ab|cd} but not on the separator, deleting b|c and
undoing gave the separator the tag x.  Separators of level 0, which
the undo inserts as newlines, were right.  (* 2.06: fixed; the
re-created separator loses the tags the list has off there, as text
does (t/undo-block.t). *)
30)* (2.05) Found while testing ragged trees: -getflags is set by the
program, and every widget call that extracts text ORed it with its own
flags, so the bits the manual calls reserved ("All undocumented bits
are reserved and should be 0") reached the widget's internal ones:
-getflags 0x200 made "block list" leave out the node values and the
instance data (a flag the undo uses), 0x100 added the undo's leaf
defects.  (* 2.06: fixed; the widget uses only the documented bits of
-getflags (TKT_GET_USER in tkText.h) and ignores the others
(t/getflags.t). *)
31)* (2.05) Found by the random test of -valuecommand in 2.06 (the
nodes told "new" get values): a value set, after a deletion that
dissolved a block nested in another block, on a leaf that the
dissolved block's separators made in the enclosing block does not
come back with the undo and redo of the deletion: the undo gives it
to the leaf of the re-created block at the same place (told
"restore"), and the redo, which dissolves that block again, takes it
into the undo step with that block's values; the leaf comes back
without a value.  With {xa{b|c|d}y} on line 1 (an outer block F, a
nested block G), deleting "{b" (the head of G and the leaf b) leaves
{xa|c|dy}; values set then on "c" and "dy" are on G's leaves c and d
after the undo, and nowhere in the text after the redo.  BUGS 26
fixed the case where the dissolved block is not nested (the place
does not exist after the undo, so the value waits).  Same in 2.05.
(* 2.06: fixed; the undo keeps such a value in the undo step (told
"stash") also when its place after the undo is inside a block the
deletion cut, which the undo re-creates (InCutBlock in
tkTextBTree.c), and the redo gives it back to the leaf of the
enclosing block (told "restore").  t/undo-block.t;
t/valuecommand-random.t no longer tolerates nodes left without a
value after an undo or a redo. *)
32)* (all versions) Found while testing the demo's OpLimits and
SpanTable layouts (2.07): "bbox" of a position inside a leaf of a
block (a character, a separator, the tail) was off downwards by the
top of the block in the window once the block had been drawn: the
display moves the internal lines of a drawn block to window
coordinates (IBTreeDisplayProc, for embedded windows), and the
bounding box took them as relative to the block, as before the first
display.  The drawing and the mouse (IBTreeMeasureProc subtracts the
offset) were right.  With -pady 20 and the leaf at y 30 in the block,
bbox gave y 50 below the block's top.  (* 2.07: fixed;
IBTreeBboxProc in tkTextDisp.c subtracts the offset as
IBTreeMeasureProc does.  t/regress.t. *)
33)* (all versions) Found by t/display-random.t (2.07): the tail of a
block that ends a display line (the text after the block wrapped to
the next display line) got the box Tk gives the last character of a
display line, reaching to the right edge of the window, not the box
of the block's last leaf that the manual promises ("Bounding boxes").
With {nk}dddddd in a widget 5 characters wide and -wrap char, bbox of
