up to the start of the last character of a chunk of characters.
t/regress.t. *)
35)* (all versions) Found while extending the ornaments (2.08): "mark
next" skipped a mark at the very start of the first leaf line of a
block: MarkFindNext went into the block's tree, set the segment to the
first one of the leaf line, and the loop's own step then went past
it.  "mark previous" had the same step, and besides searched only the
first leaf line of a block standing before the index, and nothing of
the line after such a block.  With "line 1 abc" and "block insert B
1.2 1.6", ".t mark set m 1.3": ".t mark next 1.0" gave "insert", and
".t mark previous insert" nothing ("mark previous 1.5" and "dump"
found m).  (* 2.08: fixed; MarkFindNext does not step after going
into a tree, MarkFindPrev searches a line with the contents of its
blocks in their place (LastMarkInLine in tkTextMark.c).  t/regress.t
compares both with the order of "dump" at every mark and index of a
line with a nested block. *)
36) (2.09: cut and paste with structure) What is provisional in "block
list -values", "block cut" and "block paste" (the design note
STRICTURES.md, sections 8 and 9, on the branch strictures-design):
- The rule for the values is fixed: a value travels with the delimiter
  that begins its owner, so a piece carries the values of the leaves
  and nodes that begin in it.  A cell that the end of a piece divides
  keeps its value on the first part, in the piece or in the block
  (STRICTURES.md 8.7: a span then goes with the first half of its
  cell).  No fixed rule is right for every type: the block type should
  be asked at each join and split, with both values at hand.  That
  call-out is not designed.
- A pasted separator keeps its depth counted from the leaves (as "block
  split" takes it).  The other choice, its place counted from the root
  (the list would carry the height of the tree it was cut from), is
  not made; the owner has not decided.
- A range that cuts a block: both commands delete as "delete" does (the
  block is dissolved), and the list has the part as a block of its own
  without the values of what begins with the block.  What a list should
  hold of a cut block is open (STRICTURES.md 8.4).
- Leaves of different depths: a cut that joins a leaf with a deeper one
  gives the shallower leaf nodes first (as every deletion); pasting the
  piece back does not take them out, so the leaf in which the range
  started can be deeper than before (172 of 3070 random ranges that cut
  no block, in trees made with block sprout).  The list has no place
  for the depth of the leaf before its first separator (STRICTURES.md
  8.9: the two halves of a separator).  The undo of the cut and of the
  paste is exact there too.
- -valuecommand: "block paste" tells nothing: the leaves and nodes it
  makes are not told "new", and the values it sets are not told (as
  for "block nodedata").
- Representation B: the lists carry the Lang parts of the values only
  (as "block list" always did); the other slots of a container do not
  travel through a list.
- "block level" to a lower depth joins nodes by the rule of "delete"
  (the start side's value, else the end side's); the type is not asked
  there either.  In a ragged tree it refuses to join a leaf's padding
  with a node (block sprout first), where "delete" gives the leaf nodes
  itself.
- A list is not checked against the place: a separator of a depth the
  tree does not have adds levels below the root; a defect the place
  does not allow is left out; the values of a separator pasted outside
  blocks are not used.
37) (2.09) The frames of split lines are drawn outside the box of
their stack and take no room, so they are clipped as all ornaments of
blocks are: to the display line and to the text area.  Seen under Xvfb
with a block {qq} in the default layout and the default font: the top
side of the frame is one row above the display line and is not drawn;
the bottom side is drawn (the line is a pixel higher than the block);
at the start of a line the left side is not drawn either.  With space
above and below the line (-spacing1, -spacing3, or a callback that
leaves room around the group) all four sides show.  From the report of
the session that wrote the feature, not checked again: the frame of a
block nested in a leaf falls on the frame of the stack around it where
the two boxes share an edge, and the two patterns mix; the split lines
are on the top rows of the lower leaf, under its text, so letters that
reach the top of their line cross them (lines for depths of 8 and more
take 3 rows and more); the placement of dashes on the right, bottom
and left sides of a frame depends on the X server.
38)* (all versions) Found while adding the stacks to the loop over the
result of a layout callback (2.09): a count that is negative was not
refused.  A list of one element with a negative count ("hide -1
leaves") ran "while (count--)" over the array of leaves and beyond it,
and a list of 7 elements after the last leaf (a stolen line) with a
negative ordinal indexed the array of stolen lines with it: the widget
crashed (SIGSEGV).  With "x aaaa\nbbbb y\n", "block insert P 1.2 2.4"
and a callback returning ([0,0,0,50,50,40,20], [-1]), the first update
was enough.  (* 2.09: fixed; both are refused with a background error,
"Negative count in return from LayoutProc", and the block gets the
default layout.  t/regress.t.  A list of 7 or 13 elements with a
negative count while leaves are left still describes all the remaining
leaves, which no document says: not changed. *)
39) (all versions) Found the same way: when an element of the result of
a layout callback is refused, the elements before it have already been
applied and stay.  The manual says that the block is then laid out as
if it had no callback, but a leaf that an earlier element moved stays
moved (outside the box of the block, which is the default one), and a
leaf that an earlier element hid stays hidden.  With "xx aa\nbb\ncc
yy\n", "block insert P 1.3 3.2" and a callback returning
([0,0,0,90,90,80,20], [1,30,30,40,40,19,14], [1,2,3]): one background
error, the block's bbox is the default one (25 4 14 42), and the first
leaf is drawn at (55,38), over the next line of the text.  The split
lines of such a block follow the display lines as they are.  Not fixed.
